.venv/
experiments/data/*
!experiments/data/MANIFEST.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
experiments/results/*.png
experiments/results/*.log
# Pools: ignore the CONTENTS (not the directory, or the negation below cannot
# re-include -- same defect class as the predictions block further down). The
# .npz pools are 47 MB and rebuildable via scripts/wsl_build_pools.sh.
experiments/results/pools/*
# EXCEPTION: the raw Dirac-3 responses are NOT regenerable without spending
# metered QPU seconds -- the same criterion that version-controls the hardware
# predictions below. 1.13 MB across 48 files. Appendix C claims they are
# retained in the repository; before F54 they were retained only locally, so
# that claim shipped false. Scanned for account identifiers before committing:
# zero findings, and the responses carry no org/account/token fields at all.
!experiments/results/pools/hw_responses/
docs/source/*.pdf
docs/papers/*.pdf
.secrets-patterns.txt
catboost_info/
kaggle.json
credentials.json
experiments/results/*.db
docs/paper/out/
# Predictions: ignore the CONTENTS (not the directory itself, or the negation
# below cannot re-include anything -- the Sprint 2 MANIFEST.json defect class).
experiments/results/predictions/*
# EXCEPTION: hardware predictions are NOT regenerable without spending metered
# QPU seconds, so they are version-controlled. Classical and proxy predictions
# stay ignored because they rebuild for free (see QUALITY_STANDARDS).
!experiments/results/predictions/cvqboost_hw_*.npz

# Smoke-run output; evidence lives in ieee_classical.json only.
experiments/results/*_smoke.json

# Run heartbeats, not evidence. They are rewritten on every run (including
# test runs), so tracking them makes CI's dirty-tree gate fire on a clean
# build. Evidence lives in the named results files, which ARE tracked.
experiments/results/*_progress.json

# Non-public material, segmented out of the working tree at Sprint 10 (F40,
# card #55) because F37 makes this repository public and a public repo
# publishes the whole tree. Both now live in a sibling folder outside the
# repository, which git cannot see.
#
# 0*.txt: team-lead working files. CLAUDE.md says commit but NEVER read them,
# so their contents were never inspected -- which is exactly why segmenting
# beats reviewing. A structural scan found contact details in two of them.
#
# fourierwall2: the prior Fourier Wall / SPECTRA work. The team lead decided
# on 2026-09-08 that it stays OUT of the challenge submission, and
# test_submission_does_not_claim_prior_paid_tier_degree3_work enforces that in
# the documents. Publishing its charts here would contradict that decision.
#
# HISTORY IS NOT REWRITTEN (explicit team-lead decision). Both remain in git
# history and a public repo exposes history. Accepted: the exposure is the
# team lead's own contact details and prior-work references, not credentials.
0*.txt
experiments/reference/fourierwall2/

# Internal strategy notes, segmented at Sprint 11 (team-lead decision
# 2026-09-09) before the repository goes public. A reviewer-style teardown
# scoring a named competitor, and the positioning notes that go with it, are
# working material rather than evidence: nothing in the proposal, appendix or
# team profile cites either. Retained privately alongside the other segmented
# material. Attributions to that competitor's PUBLIC work stay in the
# requirements matrix and the frozen preregistration, because crediting public
# prior work is the honest thing to do and the freeze forbids editing it.
docs/trueloop-teardown.md
docs/thesis-candidates.md
